{
  "scene": {
    "id": "check-pilot4",
    "language": "All right, hello. Today, I will be showing you how checking works. So, right now, I'm facing the goal and our goal is to get the ball into the goal. But right now, if I go ahead and... have the opponent[0] move towards me, [1]then this is not an advantage- advantageous position for us to move the ball into the goal. So what we're gonna need to do is something called checking. Checking is when we move diagonally to try to adjust my position relative to the opponent, so then you, the teammate, can have a more advent- advantageous, or I can be in a more advantageous position for my teammate, which is you, to pass me the ball. I can then choose to either pass the ball or turn around and dribble it to the goal. So let's see. [2]I'm going[3] to... [4](breathing) All right, so now I'm in this position with the opponent in front of me. I moved diagonally from my original position. However, you, the teammate, are not behind me anymore, and now you're like kind of in front of me. So, in this position, I would judge that it is not the best case scenario for me to move around the opponent and try to go to the goal. So now I'm gonna pass it to you. [5][6]And then since you are now to the side of the opponent and you have a direct path to the goal, you can then go and dribble it straight to the goal. And that is how checking can be used advantageously.[7]",
